// @run
// @log 1
// @log 2
// @hint redundant type annotation: `a` is inferred as `{r: i32}[]` — remove it to lean on inference
// THE WRITE QUESTION IS ABOUT THE VALUE, NOT ABOUT THE ELEMENT REP (D821).
//
// D791's licence for the element-converting copy was `rlSlotEverWritten` — "does any list
// store or mutating list method in this program reach a list whose element REP is this
// row". That answers the right question at the wrong granularity, and this program is the
// witness: `other.push(…)` is a store through a union-box list, `b` is a different
// union-box list, and the two share a row. Master declined the copy for `b` on `other`'s
// write and emitted an invalid module — `vl check` rc 0, no diagnostics.
//
// Declining a LOWERING on a rep-scoped answer costs nothing; REFUSING a program on one is a
// false reject, and clause 2 forbids exactly that. So the licence is now a per-VALUE alias
// closure: `a` and `b` are the only handles on this list, neither is written, and the copy
// is unobservable. `other` never joins their set, so its write says nothing about them.
//
// THE SHARPENED PREDICATE IS ONLY CONSULTED WHERE THE REP ANSWER ALREADY SAID "WRITTEN", so
// it is monotone — every delivery master widened still widens, and this is the class of
// program it adds.
type Circle = { r: i32 }
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Sq
function f() {
  const other: Shape[] = [{ r: 1 }]
  other.push({ s: 2 })
  const a: Circle[] = [{ r: 7 }]
  const b: Shape[] = a
  print(b.length)
  print(other.length)
}
f()
